            <ArticleTitle>Frontal mucocele caused by an ectopic maxillary tooth.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1384-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To present a previously unreported cause of frontal mucocele.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE REPORT" NlmCategory="METHODS">A patient presented with a frontal mucocele and maxillary sinusitis. Computed tomography revealed an ectopic maxillary tooth as the cause of her signs and symptoms. Removal of the tooth by a Caldwell-Luc procedure facilitated resolution of the mucocele. Conventional treatment of mucoceles by endoscopic sinus surgery, and other rhinological sequelae of ectopic teeth, are considered.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">This is the first documented case of an ectopic tooth causing a frontal mucocele, and demonstrates how effectively the patient's symptoms resolved on removal of the tooth.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
